Stop spice-server disconnecting the first viewer of every qemu process at 60s.

MainChannelClient uses net_test_id == 0 to mean "no net test running", but
handle_pong() compares incoming pong ids against it unconditionally. The
periodic connectivity ping takes its id from rand(), and on musl the first
rand() of a process returns 0 (seed starts at 0: 0*A+1 = 1, 1 >> 33 = 0).

So the first connectivity pong carries id 0, matches the sentinel, is swallowed
by the net-test state machine ("invalid net test stage") and never reaches the
latency monitor. latency_monitor.state stays PING_STATE_WARMUP, start_ping_timer
then refuses to arm, no further ping is ever sent, and connectivity_timer kills
the client after two 30s windows with nothing received.

Only the first client of each qemu process is affected; reconnecting works.
Does not reproduce on glibc, whose first rand() is 1804289383.

IsardVDI #3376. Report upstream and drop this once it lands.

--- a/server/main-channel-client.cpp
+++ b/server/main-channel-client.cpp
@@ -337,7 +337,9 @@
 
     roundtrip = spice_get_monotonic_time_ns() / NSEC_PER_MICROSEC - ping->timestamp;
 
-    if (ping->id != priv->net_test_id) {
+    /* net_test_id 0 means no test is running; musl's first rand() is 0, so a
+     * connectivity pong carrying id 0 must not be taken for a net-test pong */
+    if (priv->net_test_id == 0 || ping->id != priv->net_test_id) {
         /*
          * channel client monitors the connectivity using ping-pong messages
          */
--- a/server/red-channel-client.cpp
+++ b/server/red-channel-client.cpp
@@ -604,7 +604,10 @@
     spice_assert(priv->latency_monitor.state == PING_STATE_NONE);
     priv->latency_monitor.state = PING_STATE_WARMUP;
     priv->latency_monitor.warmup_was_sent = false;
-    priv->latency_monitor.id = rand();
+    /* 0 collides with main-channel-client's net_test_id sentinel */
+    do {
+        priv->latency_monitor.id = rand();
+    } while (priv->latency_monitor.id == 0);
     pipe_add_type(RED_PIPE_ITEM_TYPE_PING);
     pipe_add_type(RED_PIPE_ITEM_TYPE_PING);
 }
